An edit changes what you see. A montage changes what you connect. A meme gives that connection a form you can carry into another conversation. Memetic warfare is the contest over which of those connections people remember, repeat, and use to interpret the next thing they see.

Technique	What it does
Juxtaposition	Puts two things together so the audience supplies the comparison or implied cause. The cut itself can make an argument.
Cropping and clipping	Changes the apparent meaning by changing what surrounds a moment or where it begins and ends.
Recaptioning	Gives old footage a new subject. The same reaction shot can become a joke about work, politics, or parenting.
Sound and rhythm	Assigns a mood or a role. The same entrance scored as a coronation, a horror reveal, or a pratfall tells three different stories.
Supercut	Turns scattered instances into a perceived pattern. The missing ingredient is often the denominator. How many instances went the other way?
Freeze-frame and replay	Promotes a fleeting detail into the thing the viewer is supposed to notice.
Parody and inversion	Borrows a recognizable form and reverses its meaning. Think of a fake luxury ad for something everyone finds irritating.
Reaction and remix	Makes the original clip raw material and turns someone’s response into the main event.
Naming	Gives a recurring experience a handle. Once it has a name, people start pointing out their own examples.
Template and callback	Keeps enough of the original for recognition and leaves enough open for a new punchline.
Counterexample and context restoration	Reopens a story that an edit made feel settled. A longer clip, a wider shot, or a better comparison can change the reading.

The bigger strategic distinction is winning attention, winning the interpretation, or winning the format. A striking cut can win attention. A name can win the interpretation. A format can keep spreading after people forget who made the original, because they can use it to say something of their own.

That last one is where Pantheon’s empty slots come in. Take the Beverage Goblin. A funny edit shows him handing a nurse a fourth drink on her way out the door. A reusable format is “The Beverage Goblin appears whenever ___.” Now a runner, a nurse, or an office worker can supply the scene. You’ve made a character and given people a way to point at a situation they already recognize.

A good way to read any of these is to ask what did the creator show, what did the edit make me infer, and what part would I repeat to somebody else? Those three answers can be wildly different.